    "physical_description": "Grey earthenware dish, covered with a white slip and painted in blue and green under a clear glaze. In the middle is a cinquefoil design in a medallion, from which radiate almond-shaped leaves, each enclosed in a compartment. The back of the rim is painted with radial bands in manganese-purple.",
    "summary": "A variety of boldly decorated ceramics were made in Central Asia in the 19th century. These included brightly coloured slipwares from Bukhara, Samarkand and Rishtan, as well as white-glazed wares, painted in blue and purple, from Kokand.\r\n\r\nThere is no evidence that these wares were made in continuation of an earlier local tradition. They are frequently found in Western collections, and it is likely that the West was an important market. So, although this appears to be traditional \"peasant\" pottery made by and for local communities, it may actually be a conscious attempt to capitalise on the desire for such traditional goods among modern consumers.",
